## What worked and what failed

The native screen-control connection remained unavailable on the retained macOS/tool build.
The coordinator could nevertheless launch the installed Blender 5.1.2 through its normal
file-opening command. The user supplied screenshots; guidance referred to the currently
visible window. No agent mouse selection or GUI save was performed.

The first screenshot had Blender's Quick Setup panel. The next did not; the user asked where
to find the box, and the guidance was clarified. The instruction to expect an orange point was too
specific: the user reported white, and extraction confirmed a selected point. Color alone
was not used as the selection identity.

The original opening path was saved over before the intended separate return was obtained.
The coordinator preserved the changed bytes and found a Blender backup that matched the
original 539,650-byte scene exactly. Both versions remain retained. The old opening path must
not be treated as the original merely because its name is unchanged. Historical seals and
the earlier capture join are retained; their original-path assertion no longer describes the
file currently at that path. No old report was rewritten to hide the incident.

Repeated instructions to enter the private return path did not produce a return. The effective
repair was a visible Desktop shortcut followed by Save As with the existing filename. The
coordinator preserved the actual Desktop file, recorded its digest and copied those exact bytes
to the expected selection location. That transfer is explicitly an agent copy after the user's
save, not a claim that the user saved directly to the expected private path.

Seven screenshots are retained. No reliable active-interaction duration, onboarding duration,
interruption allocation, Blender experience or independent operator identity was supplied.
The observation form remains unfilled. Receipt timestamps and the file's modification time are
retained as separate clock observations, not counted as human working time or review savings.

## Revised assisted procedure

This revises the coordinator workflow after a demonstrated impediment. It does not change the
viewer interface, create a new viewer or modify the scientific review protocol. The simpler
working-copy save route has not yet been observed with a participant.

1. **Coordinator:** verify the selected original scene, source asset, package seal and binding
   against separately retained identities. Copy the verified original into a fresh working
   directory and record the initial copy's digest. Never launch the closed original directly.
   For this capture, use the exact original preserved from backup in the current private
   checkpoint, not the changed historical opening path.
2. **Coordinator:** preserve existing app work and launch the working copy in a separate Blender
   instance with automatic scripts disabled. File access, application launch and screen control
   are distinct capabilities. When screen control is unavailable, an actual user can supply
   screenshots; do not repeat the unchanged failing native bootstrap or describe command-line
   launch as observed GUI control.
3. **Participant:** report whether points are visible. Give one instruction at a time from the
   current screen. Dismiss Quick Setup only if it is actually shown. Choose a visible point and
   report whether its highlight changes; no specific highlight color or object label is required.
4. **Participant:** use File > Save on the working copy. A copied initial scene is preparation,
   not a return. The coordinator needs the actual interaction report and saved selection. If a
   save dialog requires navigation, the observed fallback is Desktop followed by Save As; first
   verify that the destination would not overwrite an existing file.
5. **Coordinator:** preserve received bytes and the user's own report, bind the actual save path,
   and record any transport separately. Use the existing corrected extraction command with
   mandatory `--package`, expected binding digest, separately received scene size and digest,
   and a fresh output. Verify persistent original indices and original record bytes. Preserve
   empty selection, wrong capture, altered records and missing information as distinct states.

This repair requires an ordinary file copy and custody record. Its comparator is the earlier
native opening/Save As procedure, with the same capture and selection task. Its next falsifier
is a participant failing to save the working copy, an original changing, or a returned selection
failing exact record checks. The participant benefit and total effort remain unmeasured. Do not
repeat selection exercises merely to avoid the missing physical-reference work.

## Verification and trust boundary

The unchanged native adapter at main source `fd1bba47c4a69ae9514d40c4acfbf9bfb9639552` loads a
temporary copy with scripts disabled. It recovers `original_index`, verifies the complete point
population and float32 fields, then emits the selected source record and offsets. The separate
checker imports no Engine or Blender producer code. It verifies raw/PLY equality and the exact
capture-to-anchor custody join, and rejects six substituted report fields covering capture,
scene identity, raw offset, PLY offset, record bytes and raw-body identity.

That separate reader does not parse Blender or independently recover selection bits. The native
Blender parser and selected adapter remain trusted for that part. Neither checker establishes
physical synchronization, object correspondence, human independence or observation coverage.

The extraction command took 2.251 seconds with a reported macOS child peak RSS of 245,563,392
bytes. The separate raw check took 0.050 seconds with 26,279,936 bytes. These command measurements
exclude the participant's work, guidance, app startup and repair. They are not an end-to-end cost.
No unchanged broad Engine test suite was rerun for this private inspection and documentation change.

